//? Title: Conditional VAE: A Supplied Class in Both Paths
//? Subtitle: Explicit Gaussian noise, conditional Bernoulli reconstruction and target-free decoding
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
To generate different digit classes from a shared latent space, a model needs the requested
class as an input as well as variation within that class. This conditional VAE supplies the
same one-hot class to both its Gaussian posterior and its Bernoulli decoder, with explicit
posterior noise and a fixed shared standard-normal prior. A hand-checkable example isolates
both conditioning paths before the full102,932-parameter model trains for96 Adam updates on
statically thresholded MNIST. Four fixed validation draws select epoch3. Heldout negative ELBO
is193.80764 nats/image, and decode(mu) pixel MSE is0.07637 versus0.07159 for the stronger
fitting class mean. Matched-noise controls, shifted
labels, fitting-only means and unfiltered conditional priors expose what the short run learns
and loses. The required supplied class is not a predicted label, and the bound is not exact
conditional log likelihood.
)
\toc

> Conditioning both the posterior and the generator

For binary image x and supplied class c, write h(c) for its one-hot vector. The posterior
q_phi(z|x,c)=N(mu(x,c),diag(exp(l(x,c)))) receives concat(x,h(c)). Explicit noise
epsilon~N(0,I) gives z=mu+exp(l/2)⊙epsilon. The decoder receives concat(z,h(c)) and
returns logits a(z,c); sigmoid(a) gives Bernoulli probabilities. The class is required at
inference too. Its path through the posterior is separate from its path through the decoder.

[Kingma and Welling](https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114) introduce the Gaussian pathwise
estimator; [Sohn and colleagues](https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/8d55a249e6baa5c06772297520da2051-Abstract.html)
develop conditional latent-variable models for structured outputs. This migrated teaching
configuration uses digit labels and a **fixed shared** p(z)=N(0,I). It does not reproduce a
learned conditional prior or the paper's structured-output benchmark.

By Jensen's inequality,

$$
\log p_\theta(x\mid c)\ge
\mathbb E_{q_\phi(z\mid x,c)}[\log p_\theta(x\mid z,c)]
-\operatorname{KL}(q_\phi(z\mid x,c)\Vert\mathcal N(0,I)).
$$
\alttext(The conditional evidence lower bound uses the supplied class in the posterior and decoder while the prior remains a shared standard normal.)

A single explicit draw gives the training negative-ELBO estimate

$$
L=\frac1B\sum_b\left\{
\sum_{j=1}^{784}[\operatorname{softplus}(a_{bj})-x_{bj}a_{bj}]
+\frac12\sum_{d=1}^2[\mu_{bd}^2+e^{l_{bd}}-1-l_{bd}]
\right\}.
$$
\alttext(Training sums784 Bernoulli pixel NLL terms and two Gaussian KL terms, then averages images. Beta is one.)

Pixels and latent coordinates are summed, not averaged; changing those reductions changes
the effective KL weight. There is no annealing, beta tuning or hidden sample in the core forward.
One-hot labels are discrete and receive no gradient; image, noise and all parameter paths do.
With sigma=exp(l/2), the reconstruction path contributes dL/dmu=dL/dz and
dL/dl=0.5(dL/dz)⊙sigma⊙epsilon; analytic KL adds mu/B and0.5(exp(l)−1)/B.
The decoder's one-hot suffix has its own learned weights, independent of the encoder's suffix.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cvae_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
